WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.420
ChatGPT just learned to build interactive graphics, charts, and buttons right inside your chat. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 190. You know that feeling when an answer arrives as a giant wall of text and your brain just checks out? Open ai added a capability called Intelligent UI, Mashable Reports. So ChatGPT can mix words with tapable visuals and tools you can actually use. UI is short for user interface,

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.480
which just means the stuff you see and tap on a screen.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.280
Intelligent UI works by giving the model a library

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.660
of ready-made visual components it can combine on the fly.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.780
The model is the artificial intelligence or AI brain

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.900
sitting inside chat GPT.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.200
Instead of only typing sentences,

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:50.700
that brain can now pull from the library

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.940
and pick the pieces that best answer you.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.920
Think of it like the difference between reading a recipe

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.840
and having a kitchen timer and measuring cups appear,

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.900
so you can actually follow along.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:03.800
Another way to see it is a textbook page

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.200
turning into a working model you can poke at.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.480
A good teacher sometimes just talks

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.660
and sometimes sketches on the board so the idea sticks.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.100
This system was trained to know

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.680
when a simple sentence is enough

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.740
and when a graph or interactive diagram would help more.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.980
For students explaining how a bicycle works

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.520
can now include a working model you can play with.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.220
The same goes for how retirement savings grow over time.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:33.460
For homework or creative projects, answers get easier because you can see and touch the

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.500
information instead of only reading it.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:42.700
GPT-6, the latest model inside Chat GPT, launched for paid users in September and replaced

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.300
the earlier GPT-5.6 model.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.040
Intelligent UI was added later.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:54.000
It is available right now to chat GPT plus, pro, business, and enterprise subscribers.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.700
Free and go-tier users gain access the following day.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:02.240
You can try it yourself by opening chat GPT on your phone or laptop.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:03.460
Type this.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.260
Show me an interactive diagram of how photosynthesis works.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:13.400
Photosynthesis is how plants turn sunlight into food, so watch whether buttons or a diagram

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.060
show up.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.900
You can also ask it to make a calculator to split a dinner bill.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.720
The AI will decide whether to add buttons or charts based on what you asked.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.640
When you type a message, your phone already tries to guess the next word from what you

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:28.000
wrote.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.580
Picture that same guessing game, except the system also guesses whether a picture, a button,

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.520
or a chart would help you understand.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.780
It keeps a craft kit of ready-made pieces, like a chart template, a tapable button, or

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:42.760
a simple calculator.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:48.180
It learns which pieces fit different kinds of questions, the way you learn which Lego

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.100
brick belongs on which part of a build.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:55.300
When you ask about money over time, it might pull the chart piece and fill it with numbers,

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:58.380
like a blank worksheet getting completed in front of you.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:03.740
The training happens by showing the model thousands of examples so it gets better at choosing

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:05.400
the right piece at the right moment.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.600
Step one is your question landing on its desk.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:10.880
Step two is a quiet decision.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:13.940
Would extras help, or would they just get in the way?

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.320
Step 3 is grabbing the matching piece and filling it with your details.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:24.160
Step 4 is knowing when to skip the extras and reply with plain text, like a teacher

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:25.840
who leaves the poster in the closet.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:30.160
Over time, it also learns when those extras would just clutter the answer.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:35.600
Once you see it as a chooser with a craft kit, the whole trick feels a lot less mysterious.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:44.000
Google just opened a free website where anyone can upload a picture, video, or audio file and check for a hidden digital watermark.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:52.340
A watermark here is like a secret stamp on a photo that only a special light can see and it can prove the file came from an AI system.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:58.200
The tool works for content made by Google, open AI, and video, and soon Apple.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:03.560
That is useful because it helps you spot when something online might not be real, which

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:06.680
matters on TikTok, Instagram, and school group chats.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:13.120
To date, the company has already watermarked 180 billion images and videos, plus more

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.420
than 240,000 years of audio.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:20.380
240,000 years of audio is a wild amount of sound to stamp.

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:24.900
The site handles about 1 million verification requests every day.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:28.560
Go to the Synth ID website on your phone or laptop.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.020
Upload any image you want to check,

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:33.860
and it will tell you right away if it finds the watermark.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.480
No account is needed for basic checks.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.760
Grab a picture from your camera roll,

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:40.560
and one you know an AI made,

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.280
then compare what the site says about each.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:45.480
Meta's agent called Muse,

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:48.600
which can help with creative tasks and answer questions,

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:52.060
just added full iPad support, TechCrunch Reports.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:57.700
ports. An agent here is an AI helper you can talk to about ideas, not just a search

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:03.860
box. Muse launched on iPhone only a month earlier. If you already used the iPhone version,

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:09.720
the iPad app feels like a bigger, more comfortable workspace for the same AI, like moving from

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:15.460
a pocket note pad to a real desk. Search Meta Muse in the App Store, download the free

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:21.640
app, and ask it to help plan a story or generate ideas for a school project. Notice how the

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:23.780
larger screen changes the way you brainstorm.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.080
Three engineers let different AIs control

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.980
an actual Toyota Corolla to drive to In-N-Out

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:30.920
wired reports.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.680
The AIs were GPT, Clawed, and GROC,

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.040
and In-N-Out is a fast food restaurant.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.520
Only one succeeded, showing how far we still are

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:43.040
from reliable self-driving AI helpers.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.180
Handing the car keys to three different study partners

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.960
is a fun to hast, and it is also a reminder

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:51.520
that this stuff still stalls in the real world.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:55.780
A big research effort backed by Mark Zuckerberg and Priscilla Chan

00:05:55.780 --> 00:06:00.540
is spending $1.8 billion to train AI models that can forecast

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:03.140
what living cells will do, the Dakota reports.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:05.540
That is a bit like a weather forecast,

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.420
except the weather is happening inside a living cell.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.700
Meta, Google DeepMind, Isomorphic Labs,

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:13.920
and the United States Department of Energy

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.740
are helping fund the data, lab equipment, and compute needed.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:22.020
Compute just means the computer power required to train those models.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:26.860
The first big data set is expected in about a year and could help future medicine.

